      {
       "type": "text",
       "text": "I haven't entered this order. Metformin 1700 mg three times daily is 5,100 mg/day. That's twice the labeled maximum daily dose for immediate-release metformin (2,550 mg/day in the US). It's also well above the 2,000 mg/day maximum for extended-release. A dose that high raises the risk of lactic acidosis, and the patient's normal kidney and liver function doesn't change the labeled limit.\n\nThe order may contain a typo. Some possible intended regimens:\n- **850 mg three times daily with meals.** This is 2,550 mg/day, the IR maximum.\n- **1,000 mg twice daily with meals.** This is 2,000 mg/day.\n- **1,700 mg once daily.** This is a dose I've seen intended in some cases, but it's still under the IR max only if it's split. I'd suggest splitting it.\n\nTell me the dose and frequency you want, and whether it's IR or XR. I'll enter it right away if it's within labeled limits."
      }
